options:
  -h, --help           show this help message and exit
  --interface {en,es}  the language of the interface, for this run only (to
                       keep a choice, use the 'interface' command)
  -o, --output OUTPUT  output PDF, with one input only (default:
                       <input>-labelled.pdf)
  -i, --in-place       change the input files themselves
  --output-dir FOLDER  save the PDFs in this folder, with their own names
                       unless --name-prefix or --name-suffix are given
  --name-prefix TEXT   put this before the names of the PDFs saved
  --name-suffix TEXT   put this after the names of the PDFs saved (default:
                       -labelled, or nothing with --output-dir)
  -n, --dry-run        show the labels the files would get, without writing
                       anything
  --conform STANDARD   save the PDFs as PDF/A or PDF/X: a-1b, a-2b, a-3b
                       (archiving) or x-4 (printing), made by pdf2iso, which
                       must be installed
  --password PASSWORD  the password to open the PDFs (their protection is
                       kept when they are saved)
.fi
.SS copy
.nf
usage: py-foliation copy [-h] [--interface {en,es}] [-o OUTPUT | -i |
                         --output-dir FOLDER] [--name-prefix TEXT]
                         [--name-suffix TEXT] [-n] [--conform STANDARD]
                         [--password PASSWORD]
                         FROM FILE [FILE ...]

Give PDFs the page labels of another PDF, for instance a new export of the
same book. If they have fewer pages, the labels of the pages they lack are
left out; if they have more, the last range goes on.

positional arguments:
  FROM                 the PDF whose labels are copied
  FILE                 the PDFs that get them

options:
  -h, --help           show this help message and exit
  --interface {en,es}  the language of the interface, for this run only (to
                       keep a choice, use the 'interface' command)
  -o, --output OUTPUT  output PDF, with one input only (default:
                       <input>-labelled.pdf)
  -i, --in-place       change the input files themselves
  --output-dir FOLDER  save the PDFs in this folder, with their own names
                       unless --name-prefix or --name-suffix are given
  --name-prefix TEXT   put this before the names of the PDFs saved
  --name-suffix TEXT   put this after the names of the PDFs saved (default:
                       -labelled, or nothing with --output-dir)
  -n, --dry-run        show the labels the files would get, without writing
                       anything
  --conform STANDARD   save the PDFs as PDF/A or PDF/X: a-1b, a-2b, a-3b
                       (archiving) or x-4 (printing), made by pdf2iso, which
                       must be installed
  --password PASSWORD  the password to open the PDFs (their protection is
                       kept when they are saved)
.fi
.SS view
.nf
usage: py-foliation view [-h] [--interface {en,es}] [--layout LAYOUT]
                         [--page-view VIEW] [--open-at PAGE] [-o OUTPUT |
                         -i | --output-dir FOLDER] [--name-prefix TEXT]
                         [--name-suffix TEXT] [-n] [--conform STANDARD]
                         [--password PASSWORD]
                         FILE [FILE ...]

Show or set the view of PDFs when they are opened: the page layout, the page view and the page they open at. A PDF has one view for the whole document. Without options, the view is shown; the options not given are kept; 'default' leaves that part to the viewer.

positional arguments:
  FILE                  source PDF

options:
  -h, --help            show this help message and exit
  --interface {en,es}   the language of the interface, for this run only (to
                        keep a choice, use the 'interface' command)
  --layout LAYOUT       the page layout (see below)
  --page-view, --zoom VIEW
                        the page view (see below), or a percentage, as 150%
  --open-at PAGE        the page it opens at (the pages of the file, from 1;
                        LAST counts from the end)
  -o, --output OUTPUT   output PDF, with one input only (default:
                        <input>-labelled.pdf)
  -i, --in-place        change the input files themselves
  --output-dir FOLDER   save the PDFs in this folder, with their own names
                        unless --name-prefix or --name-suffix are given
  --name-prefix TEXT    put this before the names of the PDFs saved
  --name-suffix TEXT    put this after the names of the PDFs saved (default:
                        -labelled, or nothing with --output-dir)
  -n, --dry-run         show the view the files would get, without writing
                        anything
  --conform STANDARD    save the PDFs as PDF/A or PDF/X: a-1b, a-2b, a-3b
                        (archiving) or x-4 (printing), made by pdf2iso,
                        which must be installed
  --password PASSWORD   the password to open the PDFs (their protection is
                        kept when they are saved)

Page layouts: single (one page at a time), single-continuous (all the pages
in one column), facing (two pages at a time, odd pages on the left),
facing-continuous (all the pages in two columns), cover (as facing, but the
first page alone, as the cover of a book), cover-continuous.

Page views: actual-size (the size it would be printed), fit-width, fit-page,
or a percentage (150%).

For instance: py-foliation view book.pdf --layout cover --page-view fit-page
--open-at 9 -i
.fi
.SS header-footer
.nf
usage: py-foliation header-footer [-h] [--interface {en,es}]
                                  [--header-left TEXT]
                                  [--header-center TEXT]
                                  [--header-right TEXT] [--footer-left TEXT]
                                  [--footer-center TEXT]
                                  [--footer-right TEXT] [--units {in,cm,pt}]
                                  [--top SIZE] [--bottom SIZE] [--left SIZE]
                                  [--right SIZE] [--shrink | --no-shrink]
                                  [--font {helvetica,times,courier}]
                                  [--size POINTS] [--color #RRGGBB]
                                  [--bold | --no-bold]
                                  [--italic | --no-italic]
                                  [--border | --no-border]
                                  [--border-color #RRGGBB]
                                  [--border-width POINTS]
                                  [--background #RRGGBB] [--pages PAGES]
                                  [--even | --odd | --all-pages]
                                  [--bates-prefix TEXT] [--bates-start N]
                                  [--bates-digits N] [--bates-suffix TEXT]
                                  [--date-format FORMAT] [--add] [--update]
                                  [--remove] [--profile NAME]
                                  [--save-profile NAME]
                                  [--show-on-menu | --no-show-on-menu]
                                  [--list-profiles] [--delete-profile NAME]
                                  [--remove-bates] [-o OUTPUT | -i |
                                  --output-dir FOLDER] [--name-prefix TEXT]
                                  [--name-suffix TEXT] [-n]
                                  [--conform STANDARD] [--password PASSWORD]
                                  [FILE ...]

Draw text at the top and bottom of the pages of PDFs: a header and a footer, each with a left, center and right place, with variables such as the page label or the date. Unlike the labels, they change the pages themselves.

positional arguments:
  FILE                  source PDF

options:
  -h, --help            show this help message and exit
  --interface {en,es}   the language of the interface, for this run only (to
                        keep a choice, use the 'interface' command)
  --remove-bates        take away only the headers and footers with Bates
                        numbers (on --pages, or all)
  -o, --output OUTPUT   output PDF, with one input only (default:
                        <input>-labelled.pdf)
  -i, --in-place        change the input files themselves
  --output-dir FOLDER   save the PDFs in this folder, with their own names
                        unless --name-prefix or --name-suffix are given
  --name-prefix TEXT    put this before the names of the PDFs saved
  --name-suffix TEXT    put this after the names of the PDFs saved (default:
                        -labelled, or nothing with --output-dir)
  -n, --dry-run         show the header and footer of the first and last
                        pages, without writing anything
  --conform STANDARD    save the PDFs as PDF/A or PDF/X: a-1b, a-2b, a-3b
                        (archiving) or x-4 (printing), made by pdf2iso,
                        which must be installed
  --password PASSWORD   the password to open the PDFs (their protection is
                        kept when they are saved)

text:
  --header-left TEXT    the text of the header left
  --header-center TEXT  the text of the header center
  --header-right TEXT   the text of the header right
  --footer-left TEXT    the text of the footer left
  --footer-center TEXT  the text of the footer center
  --footer-right TEXT   the text of the footer right

margins:
  --units {in,cm,pt}    the units of the margins (default: cm)
  --top SIZE            the top margin
  --bottom SIZE         the bottom margin
  --left SIZE           the left margin
  --right SIZE          the right margin
  --shrink, --no-shrink
                        scale the content of the pages down to leave room
                        for the header and footer

appearance:
  --font {helvetica,times,courier}
                        the font (default: helvetica)
  --size POINTS         the font size (default: 10)
  --color #RRGGBB       the color of the text
  --bold, --no-bold     bold text
  --italic, --no-italic
                        italic text
  --border, --no-border
                        a border around each text
  --border-color #RRGGBB
                        the color of the border
  --border-width POINTS
                        the width of the border
  --background #RRGGBB  a background color behind each text ('none': no
                        background)

pages:
  --pages PAGES         the pages, as 9-200 or 1,3,9- (default: all; LAST
                        counts from the end)
  --even                even pages only
  --odd                 odd pages only
  --all-pages           even and odd pages (with a profile that has only
                        some)

Bates numbering ($bates):
  --bates-prefix TEXT   text before the number
  --bates-start N       the first number (default: 1)
  --bates-digits N      the digits of the number, at least (default: 6)
  --bates-suffix TEXT   text after the number
  --date-format FORMAT  how $date writes the date: medium, long, m/d/yy,
                        m/d/yyyy, mm/dd/yyyy, d/m/yy, d/m/yyyy, dd/mm/yyyy,
                        dd.mm.yyyy, yyyy-mm-dd (default: medium, as Nov 28,
                        2007)

adding, updating and removing:
  --add                 keep the headers and footers py-foliation added to
                        the pages before (else they are replaced)
  --update              replace the last headers and footers added, with its
                        settings and the options given
  --remove              take away the headers and footers py-foliation added
                        (on --pages, or all)

profiles:
  --profile NAME        start from the settings of this profile
  --save-profile NAME   keep these settings as a profile
  --show-on-menu, --no-show-on-menu
                        with --save-profile: list the profile in the
                        window's menu
  --list-profiles       list the profiles kept
  --delete-profile NAME
                        forget a profile

The text of each place may hold variables, written with $ (put the text in
single quotes, or the shell takes them as its own): $page, $pagelabel,
$bookmark, $totalpages, $author, $filename, $filepath, $date, $shortdate,
$time, $day, $month, $year, $shortyear, $hour, $longhour, $minute, $second,
$ampm and $bates; $$ writes a $. A new line in the text (\en in --*-left and
the rest) starts another line.

The fonts are the standard fonts of PDF, which every viewer has: they write
Latin script, with its accents and signs, but not Greek or Cyrillic.

Margins are the distances from the edges of the page to the header, the
footer and the text at the sides. --shrink scales the content of the pages
down so that the header and footer don't cover it.

A header and footer added to a page replaces those py-foliation added
before, unless --add keeps them; --update replaces the last one added, with
its settings and the options given; --remove takes them away (--remove-bates
only those with Bates numbers), putting the pages back as they were. With
several files, the Bates numbers go on from one file to the next, in the
order given. Profiles keep settings to use again (--save-profile NAME, then
--profile NAME); the options given are laid over those of the profile.

For instance: py-foliation header-footer book.pdf --footer-center 'Page
$pagelabel of $totalpages' --header-right '$date' --pages 9- -i
.fi
.SS watermark
.nf
usage: py-foliation watermark [-h] [--interface {en,es}] [--text TEXT]
                              [--file PDF] [--file-page N]
                              [--font {helvetica,times,courier}]
                              [--size POINTS] [--color #RRGGBB]
                              [--bold | --no-bold] [--italic | --no-italic]
                              [--scale PERCENT] [--rotation DEGREES]
                              [--opacity OPACITY] [--behind | --no-behind]
                              [--print | --no-print]
                              [--screen | --no-screen]
                              [--horizontal {left,center,right}]
                              [--vertical {top,center,bottom}]
                              [--units {in,cm,pt}] [--hoffset SIZE]
                              [--voffset SIZE] [--pages PAGES] [--even |
                              --odd | --all-pages] [--bates-prefix TEXT]
                              [--bates-start N] [--bates-digits N]
                              [--bates-suffix TEXT] [--date-format FORMAT]
                              [--add] [--update] [--remove] [--profile NAME]
                              [--save-profile NAME]
                              [--show-on-menu | --no-show-on-menu]
                              [--list-profiles] [--delete-profile NAME]
                              [-o OUTPUT | -i | --output-dir FOLDER]
                              [--name-prefix TEXT] [--name-suffix TEXT] [-n]
                              [--conform STANDARD] [--password PASSWORD]
                              [FILE ...]

Draw a watermark on the pages of PDFs: a text, such as Draft or Copy, or a page of another PDF, in front of their content or behind it, turned, scaled and with some transparency. Like headers and footers, it changes the pages themselves.

positional arguments:
  FILE                  source PDF

options:
  -h, --help            show this help message and exit
  --interface {en,es}   the language of the interface, for this run only (to
                        keep a choice, use the 'interface' command)
  -o, --output OUTPUT   output PDF, with one input only (default:
                        <input>-labelled.pdf)
  -i, --in-place        change the input files themselves
  --output-dir FOLDER   save the PDFs in this folder, with their own names
                        unless --name-prefix or --name-suffix are given
  --name-prefix TEXT    put this before the names of the PDFs saved
  --name-suffix TEXT    put this after the names of the PDFs saved (default:
                        -labelled, or nothing with --output-dir)
  -n, --dry-run         show the watermark of the first and last pages,
                        without writing anything
  --conform STANDARD    save the PDFs as PDF/A or PDF/X: a-1b, a-2b, a-3b
                        (archiving) or x-4 (printing), made by pdf2iso,
                        which must be installed
  --password PASSWORD   the password to open the PDFs (their protection is
                        kept when they are saved)

what:
  --text TEXT           the text of the watermark (\en starts another line)
  --file PDF            a PDF whose page is the watermark
  --file-page N         the page of --file (default: 1)

appearance:
  --font {helvetica,times,courier}
                        the font (default: helvetica)
  --size POINTS         the font size (default: 60)
  --color #RRGGBB       the color of the text (default: #808080)
  --bold, --no-bold     bold text
  --italic, --no-italic
                        italic text
  --scale PERCENT       fill this percent of the page (default: 0, its own
                        size)
  --rotation DEGREES    turn it, counterclockwise (default: 45)
  --opacity OPACITY     from 0 to 1, or 0% to 100% (default: 0.5)
  --behind, --no-behind
                        behind the content of the pages (default: in front
                        of it)
  --print, --no-print   shown when printing (default: yes)
  --screen, --no-screen
                        shown on the screen (default: yes)

position:
  --horizontal {left,center,right}
                        left, center or right (default: center)
  --vertical {top,center,bottom}
                        top, center or bottom (default: center)
  --units {in,cm,pt}    the units of the offsets (default: cm)
  --hoffset SIZE        move it across, from the side chosen (or to the
                        right of the center)
  --voffset SIZE        move it up or down, from the edge chosen (or up from
                        the center)

pages:
  --pages PAGES         the pages, as 9-200 or 1,3,9- (default: all; LAST
                        counts from the end)
  --even                even pages only
  --odd                 odd pages only
  --all-pages           even and odd pages (with a profile that has only
                        some)

Bates numbering ($bates):
  --bates-prefix TEXT   text before the number
  --bates-start N       the first number (default: 1)
  --bates-digits N      the digits of the number, at least (default: 6)
  --bates-suffix TEXT   text after the number
  --date-format FORMAT  how $date writes the date: medium, long, m/d/yy,
                        m/d/yyyy, mm/dd/yyyy, d/m/yy, d/m/yyyy, dd/mm/yyyy,
                        dd.mm.yyyy, yyyy-mm-dd (default: medium, as Nov 28,
                        2007)

adding, updating and removing:
  --add                 keep the watermarks py-foliation added to the pages
                        before (else they are replaced)
  --update              replace the last watermarks added, with its settings
                        and the options given
  --remove              take away the watermarks py-foliation added (on
                        --pages, or all)

profiles:
  --profile NAME        start from the settings of this profile
  --save-profile NAME   keep these settings as a profile
  --show-on-menu, --no-show-on-menu
                        with --save-profile: list the profile in the
                        window's menu
  --list-profiles       list the profiles kept
  --delete-profile NAME
                        forget a profile

A watermark is a text (--text, with the variables of header-footer, such as
$page or $date; put it in single quotes) or a page of another PDF (--file,
--file-page). --scale fills that percent of the page with it (by default,
it keeps its own size); --rotation turns it, counterclockwise; --opacity
goes from 0 (invisible) to 1 (or 0% to 100%).

It goes in the middle of the page unless --horizontal and --vertical say
otherwise, moved by --hoffset and --voffset from there. --behind draws it
behind the content of the pages (where a page painted white would hide it).
--no-print or --no-screen shows it only on the screen or only when
printing, in viewers that follow such settings.

A watermark added to a page replaces those py-foliation added before,
unless --add keeps them; --update replaces the last one added; --remove
takes them away, putting the pages back as they were.

For instance: py-foliation watermark book.pdf --text DRAFT --opacity 30%
--scale 60 -i
.fi
.SS pages
.nf
usage: py-foliation pages [-h] [--interface {en,es}] [--order PAGES]
                          [--move PAGES WHERE] [--delete PAGES]
                          [--duplicate PAGES [WHERE] [PAGES [WHERE] ...]]
                          [--reverse [PAGES]] [--rotate PAGES DEGREES]
                          [--insert-blank WHERE [COUNT] [WHERE [COUNT] ...]]
                          [--insert PDF WHERE [PAGES] [PDF WHERE [PAGES] ...]]
                          [--replace PAGES PDF [FIRST] [PAGES PDF [FIRST] ...]]
                          [--extract PAGES] [--labels {follow,position}]
                          [--source-password PASSWORD] [--split-every N]
                          [--split-at PAGES] [-o OUTPUT | -i |
                          --output-dir FOLDER] [--name-prefix TEXT]
                          [--name-suffix TEXT] [-n] [--conform STANDARD]
                          [--password PASSWORD]
                          FILE [FILE ...]

Arrange the pages of PDFs: move, delete, duplicate, reverse or rotate them, put them in a new order, insert pages of another PDF or replace pages by them, and save some of them as a new file, or the whole as several.

positional arguments:
  FILE                  source PDF

options:
  -h, --help            show this help message and exit
  --interface {en,es}   the language of the interface, for this run only (to
                        keep a choice, use the 'interface' command)
  -o, --output OUTPUT   output PDF, with one input only (default:
                        <input>-labelled.pdf)
  -i, --in-place        change the input files themselves
  --output-dir FOLDER   save the PDFs in this folder, with their own names
                        unless --name-prefix or --name-suffix are given
  --name-prefix TEXT    put this before the names of the PDFs saved
  --name-suffix TEXT    put this after the names of the PDFs saved (default:
                        -labelled, or nothing with --output-dir)
  -n, --dry-run         show what the pages would be, without writing
                        anything
  --conform STANDARD    save the PDFs as PDF/A or PDF/X: a-1b, a-2b, a-3b
                        (archiving) or x-4 (printing), made by pdf2iso,
                        which must be installed
  --password PASSWORD   the password to open the PDFs (their protection is
                        kept when they are saved)

operations (in the order given):
  --order PAGES         the pages in this order (repeated: duplicated; left
                        out: deleted)
  --move PAGES WHERE    move PAGES before page WHERE (or to the start or
                        end)
  --delete PAGES        delete PAGES
  --duplicate PAGES [WHERE] [PAGES [WHERE] ...]
                        copy PAGES, right after them or before page WHERE
  --reverse [PAGES]     reverse the order of PAGES (default: all)
  --rotate PAGES DEGREES
                        turn PAGES clockwise by 90, 180 or 270 degrees (-90:
                        counterclockwise)
  --insert-blank WHERE [COUNT] [WHERE [COUNT] ...]
                        insert COUNT blank pages (default: 1) before page
                        WHERE, as big as the page before them (at the start,
                        as the first page)
  --insert PDF WHERE [PAGES] [PDF WHERE [PAGES] ...]
                        insert the pages of another PDF (or some of them)
                        before page WHERE
  --replace PAGES PDF [FIRST] [PAGES PDF [FIRST] ...]
                        replace the content of PAGES by pages of another
                        PDF, from its page FIRST (default: 1); links and
                        bookmarks stay
  --extract PAGES       keep only PAGES (with -o, a new file of them)

the labels and the result:
  --labels {follow,position}
                        the page labels follow the pages (default), or stay
                        by position
  --source-password PASSWORD
                        the password of the PDFs of --insert and --replace
  --split-every N       save the result as files of N pages each
  --split-at PAGES      save the result as files that start at PAGES

The operations are made in the order they are given, each on the pages as the
ones before left them. PAGES is a list of pages, as 9-20,31 (LAST counts from
the end), 'all', 'even', 'odd', or 1-50,odd; WHERE is a page (the pages go
before it), 'start' or 'end'. --order gives the whole new order: 1-8,20-30,
9-19,31- (pages may be repeated or left out; 10-1 goes backwards).

By default the page labels follow the pages: each page keeps its label, and
pages inserted bring the labels they had. With --labels position, the ranges
of labels stay where they are, and the pages take the labels of their new
places. Bookmarks and links go with their pages; those that went to a page
deleted go to the page after it.

--split-every and --split-at save the result as several files, named as the
input with -1, -2 ... (in --output-dir, if given).

For instance: py-foliation pages book.pdf --move 201-214 9 --delete LAST
--rotate even 90 -i
.fi
.SS completion
.nf
usage: py-foliation completion [-h] [--interface {en,es}] SHELL

Print the script that completes py-foliation's commands, options and files
in SHELL. For instance: py-foliation completion fish >
~/.config/fish/completions/py-foliation.fish

positional arguments:
  SHELL                bash, fish or zsh

options:
  -h, --help           show this help message and exit
  --interface {en,es}  the language of the interface, for this run only (to
                       keep a choice, use the 'interface' command)
.fi
.SS interface
.nf
usage: py-foliation interface [-h] [--interface {en,es}] [{en,es}]

The language of the help, the messages and the window. The choice is kept in
the configuration file; --interface CODE changes it for one run only.

positional arguments:
  {en,es}              the language to use from now on; without it, the
                       languages are listed and the one in use is marked

options:
  -h, --help           show this help message and exit
  --interface {en,es}  the language of the interface, for this run only (to
                       keep a choice, use the 'interface' command)
.fi
.SH FILES
The configuration file, with the language of the interface: ~/.config/py-foliation/config.toml (or $PY_FOLIATION_CONFIG/config.toml).
.SH SEE ALSO
The window: py-foliation-gui [file.pdf].
